    "title": "Michigan basketball eliminates Tennessee, advances to Final Four",
    "text": "March 28, 2026Updated March 29, 2026, 4:54 p.m. ETNo. 6 seed Tennessee (25-12) was eliminated in the NCAA Tournament Elite Eight. No. 1 seed Michigan (35-3) defeated the Vols, 95-62, on Sunday at United Center in Chicago, Illinois.Tennessee advanced to its third consecutive Elite Eight under 11th-year head coach Rick Barnes.Ja'Kobi Gillespie led the Vols in scoring with 21 points. He converted 8-of-22 field goal attempts, 4-of-13 three-point attempts and 1-of-1 free throw attempts.The Vols and Wolverines each recorded 42 rebounds in the contest. Michigan converted 27-of-37 free throw attempts, while Tennessee converted 9-of-17.Yaxel Lindeborg led Michigan in scoring with 27 points.Vols Wire provided live game updates and are listed below. Final - Michigan 95, Tennessee 62Jaylen Carey receives a dead ball technical foul for Tennessee with 14:02 in the second half, his fourth personal foul.Nate Ament fouled out for Tennessee with 2:16 in the second half. Ja'Kobi Gillespie's dunk for Tennessee in the second half Halftime - Michigan 48, Tennessee 26Jaylen Carey led Tennessee in scoring during the first half with eight points.Yaxel Lendeborg led Michigan in scoring during the first half with 15 points.Tennessee outrebounded Michigan, 25-22, in the first half.Fouls: Michigan 9, Tennessee 10+Aday Mara recorded his second foul for Michigan with 15:05 in the first half.Felix Okpara recorded his second foul for Tennessee with 14:01 in the first half.Jaylen Carey recorded his second foul for Tennessee with 10:52 in the first half.Morez Johnson Jr. recorded his second foul for Michigan with 10:20 in the first half.Felix Okpara recorded his third foul for Tennessee with 9:19 in the first half.Michigan went on a 21-0 run for five minutes, 43 seconds.DeWayne Brown II recorded his second foul for Tennessee with 3:08 in the first half.Roddy Gayle Jr. recorded his second foul for Michigan with 2:21 in the first half. Yaxel Lendeborg's reverse in the first half for Michigan Tennessee-Michigan basketball game officiating crewReferee: Gregory NixonReferee: Bart LenoxReferee: Jeffrey Anderson Tennessee's starting lineup versus MichiganJa'Kobi Gillespie, Bishop Boswell, Nate Ament, J.P. Estrella and Felix Okpara are the Vols' starting five on Sunday. Tennessee versus Michigan basketball game injury reportCade Phillips is out for Tennessee, while L.J. Carson and Winters Grady are out for Michigan in the Elite Eight. Pregame Big Orange SundayTennessee playing for Final Four appearanceWhat channel is Tennessee vs. Michigan on?The Tennessee versus Michigan basketball game will be televised by CBS.Channel: CBSAnnouncers: Andrew Catalon (play-by-play), Steve Lappas (analyst) and Evan Washburn (reporter)Livestream: Watch live on CBSDate: Sunday, March 29Tipoff time: 2:15 p.m. EDTLocation: United Center (Chicago, Illinois)Watch Elite Eight games liveTennessee-Michigan basketball game prediction in Elite EightAhead of Sunday's basketball game, Michigan has a 68.2 percent chance to defeat the Vols, according to ESPN's Matchup Predictor. Nolan Analytics projects the Wolverines to defeat Tennessee, 77-70.Final Four basketball appearances all time for every SEC schoolRick Barnes on 2026 Tennessee basketball\"God has blessed me with a great job, good players,\" Barnes said after Tennessee defeated Iowa State in the Sweet 16. \"We have 11 new ones this year. We do have a standard on a lot of things. It's not just what we do on the basketball court. You know, we're pretty much a no-nonsense program. We talk about that through the recruiting process.\"We tell everyone it's going to be tough. We want them to come and want to help them live their dreams, and along the way we would love to have a chance to play for a national championship. It goes back to the entire program and just blessed that God has put so many wonderful people around us, and they work really hard. They work really hard.\"Game dayNorth Carolina State to interview Justin Gainey in ChicagoTennessee practices ahead of playing MichiganTennessee is only basketball program with four consecutive Sweet 16, three consecutive Elite Eight appearances2026 NCAA Tournament Elite Eight schoolsTennessee basketball Elite Eight ticketsTennessee basketball history in NCAA TournamentSweet 16 appearances: 1967, 1981, 2000, 2007, 2008, 2010, 2014, 2019, 2023, 2024, 2025, 2026Elite Eight appearances: 2010, 2024, 2025, 2026Final Four appearances: NoneThe Vols are 34-28 all time in the NCAA Tournament, including a 15-7 record under head coach Rick Barnes. Barnes is 36-29 all time in NCAA Tournament games as a head coach.Tennessee-Michigan all-time basketball seriesMichigan leads the basketball series against Tennessee, 7-5.Tennessee's all-time leader in NCAA Tournament blocks Felix OkparaMichigan Forward Yaxel Lendeborg's NBA draft projection2025-26 Tennessee basketball schedule, resultsTennessee basketball record: 25-11 (11-7 SEC)Oct. 26 – DUKE (exhibition - L, 83-76)Nov. 3 – MERCER (W, 76-61)Nov. 8 – NORTHERN KENTUCKY (W, 95-56)Nov. 12 – NORTH FLORIDA (W, 99-66)Nov. 17 – RICE (W, 91-66)Nov. 20 – TENNESSEE STATE (W, 89-60)Nov. 24 – vs. Rutgers (W, 85-60)Nov. 25 – vs. Houston (W, 76-73 - Las Vegas, Nevada)Nov. 26 – vs. Kansas (L, 81-76 - Las Vegas, Nevada)Dec. 2 – at Syracuse (L, 62-60)Dec. 6 – vs. Illinois (L, 75-62 - Nashville, Tennessee)Dec. 16 – LOUISVILLE (W, 83-62)Dec. 21 – GARDNER-WEBB (W, 94-52)Dec. 30 – SOUTH CAROLINA STATE (W, 105-54)Jan. 3 – at Arkansas (L, 86-75)Jan. 6 – TEXAS (W, 85-71)Jan. 10 – at Florida (L, 91-67)Jan. 13 – TEXAS A&M (W, 87-82 2OT)Jan. 17 – KENTUCKY (L, 80-78)Jan. 24 – at Alabama (W, 79-73)Jan. 28 – at Georgia (W, 86-85 OT)Jan. 31 – AUBURN (W, 77-69)Feb. 3 – OLE MISS (W, 84-66)Feb. 7 – at Kentucky (L, 74-71)Feb. 11 – at Mississippi State (W, 73-64)Feb. 14 – LSU (W, 73-63)Feb. 18 – OKLAHOMA (W, 89-66)Feb. 21 – at Vanderbilt (W, 69-65)Feb. 24 – at Missouri (L, 73-69)Feb. 28 – ALABAMA (L, 71-69)March 3 – at South Carolina (W, 78-59)March 7 – 